\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Turning a facet into a puzzle move}

\p{We now use the [facet extension lemma](frap-0008) to define a local move.
Let #{P} be a finite regular abstract #{n}-polytope, #{n\geq3}. Finiteness
lets us speak about finite permutations; the extension argument itself did
not need it. We follow Marchetti's incidence construction, retaining both
entries of a pair \citet{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik}.}

\p{For a regular rank-#{m} facet, choose a base flag and its distinguished
involutions #{\rho_0,\ldots,\rho_{m-1}}. Its rotation subgroup is
#{\Gamma^+=\langle\rho_{j-1}\rho_j:1\leq j<m\rangle}.
Equivalently it consists of even-length words in the distinguished
involutions: every pair #{\rho_i\rho_j} is a product of adjacent pairs,
by cancellation along the index interval. Thus this subgroup is normal,
and changing the base flag conjugates the generators without changing it.
We do not require it to have index two for every abstract polytope.
For triangles and squares it is the usual cyclic rotation group.}

\subtree{
  \refcardt{Definition}{The incidence set and a facet move}{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik}{
    \p{Put #{P^\circ=P\setminus\{F_{-1},F_n\}} and}
    ##{\mathcal R(P)=\{(F,G)\in P^\circ\times P^\circ:F\leq G\}.}
    \p{Choose a facet #{H} and a rotation #{\alpha} of #{H/F_{-1}}.
    Write #{\widehat\alpha} for its unique extension to a shape
    automorphism fixing #{H}. Define}
    ##{\mu_{H,\alpha}(F,G)=
    \begin{cases}
    (\widehat\alpha(F),\widehat\alpha(G)),&F\leq H,\\
    (F,G),&F\not\leq H.
    \end{cases}}
    \p{The facet is part of the move's name. The same shape automorphism
    may stabilize several facets, which give different local moves.
    The \em{Rubik group} is the subgroup of #{\operatorname{Sym}(\mathcal R(P))}
    generated by these moves for all facets and facet rotations.}
  }
}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Lemma}
  \title{Every facet move is a permutation}
  \p{The inverse of #{\mu_{H,\alpha}} is #{\mu_{H,\alpha^{-1}}}.}
  \proof{
    \p{An automorphism preserves proper faces and the order relation,
    so the moving branch remains in #{\mathcal R(P)}. Since
    #{\widehat\alpha(H)=H}, the condition #{F\leq H} holds exactly when
    #{\widehat\alpha(F)\leq H}. Thus the moving and fixed subsets are
    preserved separately. On the moving subset the extension of
    #{\alpha^{-1}} is #{\widehat\alpha^{-1}} by uniqueness, and cancels
    the first map. On the fixed subset both maps are the identity.}
  }
}

\p{A pair is a labelled incidence position. A configuration can be read as the
permutation carrying each initial label to its current position; a new move
acts on the left. A solved visible coloring can identify several labelled
configurations, so the choice of labels and observations matters.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Example}
  \title{Two turns of a tetrahedron}
  \p{Use vertex subsets as faces. For #{H=123}, take
  #{\widehat\alpha=(123)}, fixing vertex #{4}. Then}
  ##{(1,124)\longmapsto(2,234),\qquad
      (12,124)\longmapsto(23,234),\qquad
      (4,124)\longmapsto(4,124).}
  \p{The last pair is fixed although the shape symmetry changes its second
  entry. The moving condition uses the location, not the color face. For
  #{K=124}, take #{\widehat\beta=(124)}. Applying the first move and then
  the second sends}
  ##{(1,124)\xrightarrow{\mu_{H,\alpha}}(2,234)
       \xrightarrow{\mu_{K,\beta}}(4,134).}
  \p{Reversing their order gives #{(1,124)\mapsto(2,124)\mapsto(3,234)}.
  The moves therefore do not commute. Each has order three: its cube is
  the identity on both branches, and its displayed action is nontrivial.}
}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Example}
  \title{One ordinary cube face turn}
  \p{Realize the cube as #{[-1,1]^3}. Let #{H} be the facet #{z=1};
  the shape rotation #{q(x,y,z)=(y,-x,z)} is a clockwise quarter-turn
  when viewed from positive #{z}, with #{x} rightward and #{y} upward.
  Its restriction to #{H} supplies our move. For example, the corner
  #{(1,1,1)} goes to #{(1,-1,1)}, and its color on #{y=1} goes to the
  color position on #{x=1}. The corresponding corner at #{z=-1} is fixed
  by the puzzle move, although the global map #{q} moves it.}
  \p{If the second entry is restricted to facets, the incidence positions
  number #{8\cdot3+12\cdot2+6=54}: three per corner, two per edge, and
  one per face center. The turn moves twelve corner stickers and eight
  edge stickers. It fixes the other thirty-four positions, including the
  unmarked center of the turned face. This is the usual outer-face action
  on visible stickers of a #{3\times3\times3} cube in a fixed frame.}
  \p{The full incidence set has #{8\cdot7+12\cdot3+6=98} elements:
  above each vertex are that vertex, three edges, and three facets;
  above each edge are itself and two facets. The facet-colored subset is
  invariant under every move. We use its restriction to compare with
  ordinary stickers; no claim about a faithful restriction for all abstract
  polytopes is needed. Marked center orientations and inner-layer turns
  are additional data, absent from this comparison.}
}

\p{Both examples distinguish the global extension from its local use. The
extension transports the incident labels consistently; the facet condition
decides which locations participate. Next we separate a piece's destination
from the orientation recorded in its local frame.}
